5. Stack Using Array

Suppose stack size is 5.

Index:   0    1    2    3    4

       ┌────┬────┬────┬────┬────┐
       │ 10 │ 20 │ 30 │    │    │
       └────┴────┴────┴────┴────┘
                       ↑
                      TOP

Usually we maintain a variable:

top

If stack is empty:

top = -1

After inserting 10:

top = 0

After inserting 20:

top = 1

After inserting 30:

top = 2

So:

array[top]

gives the current top element.

6. PUSH Algorithm

For an array-based stack:

if top == MAX - 1
    Stack Overflow
else
    top = top + 1
    stack[top] = item

Understand the logic:

Is stack full?
       ↓
     YES → Overflow
       ↓ NO
Increase top
       ↓
Insert item
7. POP Algorithm
if top == -1
    Stack Underflow
else
    item = stack[top]
    top = top - 1

Meaning:

Is stack empty?
       ↓
     YES → Underflow
       ↓ NO
Take top element
       ↓
Decrease top
8. VERY IMPORTANT: Overflow vs Underflow

This is a common MCQ trap.

Stack Overflow

Trying to insert into a full stack.

FULL STACK
   ↓
PUSH()
   ↓
OVERFLOW
Stack Underflow

Trying to delete from an empty stack.

EMPTY STACK
   ↓
POP()
   ↓
UNDERFLOW
Memory trick

OVER = Full
UNDER = Empty

9. Time Complexity

For a normal array-based stack:

Operation	Complexity
PUSH	O(1)
POP	O(1)
PEEK	O(1)
IsEmpty	O(1)

Why?

Because we operate only on the top.

We don't need to search through all elements.

10. Stack vs Queue

This is extremely important.

Stack	Queue
LIFO	FIFO
Last In First Out	First In First Out
Insertion at TOP	Insertion at REAR
Deletion at TOP	Deletion at FRONT
Example

Stack:

A
B
C

POP → A

Actually if insertion order is:

A → B → C

then:

POP → C

because C entered last.

Queue:

A → B → C

then:

DELETE → A

because A entered first.

Memory trick

Stack = plates

Queue = people waiting in line

11. Applications of Stack

Very important for exams.

Stacks are used in:

1. Function calls

When a function calls another function, information is maintained using a call stack.

Example:

main()
  ↓
function A()
  ↓
function B()

The most recently called function finishes first.

2. Recursion

Example:

factorial(5)

The function calls itself.

The function calls are stored in the stack.

3. Expression evaluation

Stacks are heavily used for:

Infix
Prefix
Postfix
4. Parentheses matching

Example:

{ [ ( ) ] }

Stack can be used to check whether brackets are correctly matched.

5. Undo operation

Many systems use stack-like behavior for:

Action 1
Action 2
Action 3

Undo removes the most recent action first.

12. Infix, Prefix and Postfix

This is a high-priority topic.

Suppose:

A + B
Infix

Operator is between operands.

A + B

This is what humans commonly use.

Prefix

Operator comes before operands.

+ A B

Also called:

Polish notation

Postfix

Operator comes after operands.

A B +

Also called:

Reverse Polish notation

13. One Easy Memory Trick
INFIX
A + B
  ↑
middle

PREFIX
+ A B
↑
front

POSTFIX
A B +
    ↑
  back

So:

PRE = before
POST = after

